  "fellowshipPage": {
    "about": "What the hall's page in the roll shows, by what has become of the hall: the clue the recorder gives, one of two for each cause.",
    "discriminator": "cause",
    "groups": {
      "proud": [
        "The keeper's tick is there for every quarter back to the founding, and the last six are in a larger hand than the first. At the foot a line has been added in the keeper's own writing: 'Answered. Nothing owing.' The head has not seen it.",
        "Every quarter is ticked, and every tick is correct, and under the last one a line in the keeper's hand has been scored across and written again, harder: 'The hall keeps its own counsel.' It has been written three times."
      ],
      "sold": [
        "The tally-mark is there, and it is a good hand, but the ink is a day too new for the quarter it claims. Across the margin, small and level, a stranger's counting-mark: three upright strokes and a bar, the sign a factor makes when he has bought a thing outright.",
        "The quarter is ticked in a hurry, and under it, rubbed out and half legible, the figure of a sum paid to the hall: paid in, not owed, and the date is the week the dues were due."
      ],
      "lapsed": [
        "The column ends with a keeper's name and the line drawn under it that the fellowship draws under the dead. Below it a second name has been written in another hand and never ticked: the keeper's apprentice, entered as successor, and never confirmed.",
        "The last tick is the winter's. After it the hall's column is blank except for a name, written small and low, and the word 'heir' beside it in the dead keeper's own hand, and nothing has been done with it since."
      ],
      "fallen": [
        "Every tick is there, steady and square. The last is not: the stroke wanders off the line, and the mark beside it is a blot, as if the hand had been taken from the page. Nothing was written after.",
        "The quarter's tick is made, and made in a hurry, and the dues column beside it holds only a number and the words 'by the first one who comes'. The hand is a good hand, and it is not steady."
      ]
    }
  },
  "fellowshipReplyYes": {
    "discriminator": "any",
    "groups": {
      "default": [
        "The drawer comes out with a hazel rod in it, split lengthways and notched across. 'A stranger sold it me for a song, and I paid the song, and I have been turning it over since. It is not a thing a shop can sell. If you want it, it is",
        "A split hazel rod comes up out of the till, wrapped in sacking. 'That. A woman in a hurry sold it me. She said it was worth nothing, and she was wrong, and I knew it as she said it. I will let you have it for"
      ]
    }
  },
  "fellowshipReplyNo": {
    "discriminator": "any",
    "groups": {
      "default": [
        "The shopkeeper turns out the drawer under the counter, and the shelf behind it, and shakes their head. 'No. Nothing of the kind. I would remember a stick with notches.'",
        "The shopkeeper listens to the whole of it and thinks a while. 'A tally? Hazel? No. I have bought a good deal of nothing from strangers this season, but not that.'"
      ]
    }
  },
